                <pb facs="0563" n="553" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg553"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.51/3418: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Finley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-10T11:00:00-05:00">August 10, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 12:02 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. My telegram No. 62, August 9, 1 p.m.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d554-f1"/> De la
                    Rue at Leger’s request is again writing to Eberstadt in an endeavor to elucidate
                    the viewpoint of the Haitian Government and to find out whether he is prepared
                    to proceed on any other basis than that which he seems most unwittingly to have
                    envisaged. Pending receipt of his reply Leger told De la Rue that he would
                    prefer not to proceed with protocol negotiations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Finley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
